course: dictionary
lesson: 7276
title: Context clues with a sewing word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A pincushion is a small, firm pad, stuffed so that you can push pins and needles into it. It keeps sharp things safe while you sew. «A girl in Toronto counts her sewing needles back into the pincushion» tells you where the needles go and why she counts them.
explain: You can use the words around an unknown word to check its meaning. The word pincushion is made of two smaller words: pin and cushion. A cushion is soft and firm, and a pin is sharp, so a pincushion is a cushion for pins. Put your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Writers also use the word to paint a picture. «Like a pincushion» describes something full of sharp things, such as a hedgehog covered in spikes. The words around it show you the meaning, even if you have never seen one.
fact: A pincushion is a small, firm, stuffed pad for keeping pins and needles safe.
fact: «Tomato pincushion» and «wrist pincushion» are names for pincushions shaped like a tomato or worn on the wrist.
fact: Pupils use a pincushion in crafts and design lessons so that sharp pins are not left lying about.
fact: Needles go back into the pincushion and are counted, so none are left to prick people or pets.
fact: «Like a pincushion» means full of sharp things.
exercise: What is a pincushion? | A tool for cutting cloth | *A small, firm, stuffed pad for keeping pins and needles safe | A soft pillow for resting your head
exercise: «Most needle pricks come from pushing the needle towards your own fingers.» Where should a needle go when you have finished sewing? | In your pocket | Dropped in the bin with the scraps | Left on the carpet | *In a pincushion
exercise: «Like a pincushion» describes something full of sharp things. Which thing could a writer compare to a pincushion? | A smooth, warm pebble | A calm, still lake | *A hedgehog covered in spikes | A bowl of soft jelly
test: What should you do with a needle found on the floor? | Throw it at the cat | Stick it in your sleeve | *Put it in a pincushion or give it to a grown-up | Step over it and leave it
test: Why are sewing needles counted back into the pincushion? | *So none are left to prick people or pets | To make the pincushion heavier | To tell the time | So they can be painted
exam: Which sentence uses pincushion to mean a small, firm pad for pins? | *Knitting and sewing needles are kept in a pincushion and counted back | Sam ate a pincushion for his tea | The pincushion was bright blue and very loud
